# Header rules in the portable format read by Cloudflare Workers static assets.
#
# netlify.toml also exists and its headers are INERT — the host has been a
# Cloudflare Worker since before 14 Aug 2026 and only this file is read. It is
# not kept in step any more; CLAUDE.md records it as history pending deliberate
# removal. Changing netlify.toml changes nothing a reader will ever receive.

# EVERY PAGE REVALIDATES. Reported 17 Aug 2026: clicking the masthead returned
# the home page as it had been before a redesign. The href was correct and the
# deployed file was correct — the reader's browser simply never asked again.
#
# Only /dungeons/, /raids/, /learn/ and /tools/ had a revalidate rule. The site
# ROOT had none, and neither did /items/, /named/, /archive/, /sets/, or the
# top-level pages, so the single most-visited page on the site was the one most
# free to go stale. With no Cache-Control at all a browser falls back to
# heuristic caching off Last-Modified, which for a page edited days ago means
# hours.
#
# This is the default now, and the four rules further down are redundant with
# it; they are kept because a future narrowing of this rule should not silently
# un-fix the guides.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Assets may cache, because the one that changes with every redesign now
# carries a content hash in its URL (assets/site.css?v=…, see _partials.py), so
# a changed stylesheet is a different cache key rather than a stale hit. The
# unhashed assets here — zone plans, share cards, the vendored three.js — change
# rarely, and an hour of staleness on them costs a reader nothing.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# THE PUBLIC DATA CONTRACT.
# These files exist to be read by other people's tools, from other origins, so
# they need CORS or the whole point is lost — a browser-side consumer would get
# nothing but an opaque failure. Verified live: this host honours _headers.
#
# Cached for an hour and revalidated. The datasets carry a content `hash` so a
# consumer can cache on that instead and skip the transfer entirely.
/data/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

# Guides change several times a day. Never let a stale page stick.
/dungeons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/raids/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/learn/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/tools/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
